4.text:
In the inverse form let

* $P_{D}(Q)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q}\ge 0$
* $P_{S}(Q)=\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)\qquad Q>\root3\of2 $

so that on the market

$P^{*}=P_{D}(Q^{*})=P_{S}(Q^{*})$ ,

\begin{equation*}
\ln \!\left(Q^{*3}-2\right)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\;,
\qquad Q^{*}>\root3\of2 .
\end{equation*}

The equation is monotone in $Q^{*}$ on $\root3\of2<Q<\infty$.
Numerically

* $Q^{*}\simeq 4.69\!-\!4.70$,

\begin{equation*}
Q^{*}\approx 4.69 ,
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}= \ln\!\left(Q^{*3}-2\right)=18e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7
\end{equation*}

i.e. $P^{*}\simeq 4.62$ , $P^{*}Q^{*}\simeq 2.17\times10^{1}$.
In $Q$-space

\begin{equation*}
{\rm CS}= \int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)dq-P^{*}Q^{*},
\qquad 
{\rm PS}= P^{*}Q^{*}-\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}(q)dq ,
\end{equation*}

$ q_{0}=\root3\of2$ being the (finite) lower limit of the
inverse supply.  With $t=\arctan q$

\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)dq
 =18\int_{0}^{t^{*}}e^{-t}\sec^{2}t\,dt,\qquad 
t^{*}=\arctan Q^{*}\approx1.36 ,
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
W(Q^{*})=
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}} \! \big[\,P_{D}(q)-P_{S}(q)\,\big]dq
     ={\rm CS}+{\rm PS}
\end{equation*}

is the usual gross surplus.  Numerically

\begin{equation*}
I_{D}\;:=\;\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}18e^{-\arctan q}dq
      \;\approx\;18\times1.80\approx 3.24\!-\!3.25\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
{\rm CS}=I_{D}-P^{*}Q^{*}\approx32.4-21.7\approx 1.0\!-\!1.1\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}

about $10.7$–$10.8$ in the conventional units.
For the (finite) supply integral

\begin{equation*}
I_{S}(Q^{*})=\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right)dq
          \approx 9.6\!-\!9.9,
\end{equation*}

e.g. with the usual $P_{S}=0$ lower limit $q_{0}^{0}=\root3\of3$
$I_{S}^{0}\approx9.89$, and

\begin{equation*}
{\rm PS}=P^{*}Q^{*}-I_{S}\approx21.7-9.7\approx 1.2\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}

about $11.8$–$12.0$.  Hence on the usual gross-surplus
convention

\[
W^{*}= {\rm CS}+{\rm PS}
     =\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}\!\big[P_{D}(q)-P_{S}(q)\big]dq
     \approx 2.2\!-\!2.3\times10^{1}
\]

about $22$–$23$ monetary units, e.g. $W^{*}\simeq 22.5\!-\!22.8$
with the $q_{0}=\root3\of 2$ limit, $W^{*}\approx22.7$,
${\rm CS}\approx10.8$, ${\rm PS}\approx12.0$, $Q^{*}\approx4.69$,
$P^{*}\approx4.62$.  With the customary $P_{S}=0$ truncation
$q_{0}^{0}=\root3\of3$ this is $W^{*}\approx22.5$, ${\rm CS}\approx10.8$,
${\rm PS}\approx11.8$, the familiar $I_{D}\approx32.4$ being the total
area under inverse demand up to $Q^{*}$.  The quoted numbers are
stable to the usual numerical tolerance; using the $q_{0}=\root3\of2$
limit the often quoted figure is $W^{*}\!\approx\!22.7$ with $Q^{*}\!\approx\!4.7$
and $P^{*}\!\approx\!4.6$. ∎

10.text:
With the usual inverse functions

$$P_{S}(Q)=\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)\qquad  Q^{3}>2~,$$
$$P_{D}(Q)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q}~,$$

the competitive price is obtained from $P_{S}(Q^{\ast})=P_{D}(Q^{\ast})$,

\begin{equation*}
\ln\!\left(Q^{\ast 3}-2\right)=18\,\exp\!\Big[-\arctan Q^{\ast}\Big] .
\end{equation*}

The transcendental equation is monotone on $Q>c_{2}^{1/3}$ and

\begin{equation*}
Q^{\ast}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7 ,\qquad 
P^{\ast}= \ln\!\big(Q^{\ast 3}-2\big)=18e^{-\arctan Q^{\ast}}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7 ,
\end{equation*}
in the numerical solution

* $Q^{\ast}\simeq 4.69$, 
* $P^{\ast}\simeq \ln\big(4.69^{3}-2\big)\simeq 18e^{-\arctan 4.69}
      \simeq 4.62$

is obtained, e.g. $P^{\ast}Q^{\ast}\simeq4.62\times4.69\simeq21.7$.
Putting
\begin{equation*}
CS=\int_{0}^{Q^{\ast}}P_{D}(q)dq-P^{\ast}Q^{\ast},\qquad
PS=P^{\ast}Q^{\ast}-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{\ast}}P_{S}(q)dq ,
\end{equation*}
with the natural $P_{S}=0$ – point $P_{S}(Q_{0})=0$, $Q_{0}=3^{1/3}$,
and

\begin{equation*}
\int P_{S}(q)dq
 =q\ln\big(q^{3}-2\big)-3q
   -\frac{2}{r^{2}}\ln|q-r|
   +\frac{1}{r^{2}}\ln\!\big(q^{2}+rq+r^{2}\big)
   +\frac{2\sqrt3}{r^{2}}\arctan\frac{2q+r}{\sqrt3\,r}\!,
\end{equation*}
$r=2^{1/3}$, the producer cost between the first positive price and
$Q^{\ast}$ is

\begin{equation*}
C(Q^{\ast})=\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{\ast}}\ln\!\big(q^{3}-2\big)dq
      \approx 9.9\;,
\end{equation*}
and numerically

\begin{equation*}
I_{D}(Q^{\ast})=\int_{0}^{Q^{\ast}}18e^{-\arctan q}dq\approx 32.4\!-\!32.5,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual $0$–$Q^{\ast}$ convention

\begin{equation*}
CS=I_{D}(Q^{\ast})-P^{\ast}Q^{\ast}\approx32.5-21.7\approx1.0\!-\!1.1\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
PS=P^{\ast}Q^{\ast}-C(Q^{\ast})\approx21.7-9.9\approx1.17\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $10.8$ of consumer surplus and about $11.7$ of producer surplus.
Hence, on the usual $ \int_{0}^{Q^{\ast}}\!\big[P_{D}-P_{S}\big]dq$ convention

\begin{equation*}
W(Q^{\ast})=I_{D}(Q^{\ast})-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{\ast}}P_{S}(q)dq
        =CS+PS\;\approx\;2.2\!-\!2.3\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}
in the same monetary units as $P$, viz. $W\approx 22\!-\!23$, about
$W\approx22.5$ with the numerical values quoted above, $CS\approx10.8$
and $PS\approx11.7$, $P^{\ast}Q^{\ast}\approx21.7$.  With the customary
$Q_{0}=3^{1/3}$ the $P_{S}$–integral is $ \approx9.9$, so that
$W\simeq I_{D}(Q^{\ast})-9.9\simeq22.5$, which is the competitive total
surplus at $Q^{\ast}\!\approx\!4.69$, $P^{\ast}\!\approx\!4.62$.  This is
the $CS+PS$ total welfare commonly quoted as $ \approx 22\!-\!23$ units,
about $10.8$ consumer surplus and $11.7$ producer surplus at the
competitive equilibrium.

